  <h2>What goes on a plumbing estimate</h2>
  <p>
    A homeowner comparing three plumbers looks at the same few things: how many fixtures, what pipe
    goes in the walls, which water heater, and whether the permit is included. Those go in the
    job-specs strip under the header, so the comparison happens on your terms. The line items below
    it are grouped the way the work happens on site:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Rough-in (supply and DWV):</strong> disconnecting and capping the old fixtures, then drains,
      vents, supply lines and valve bodies for each fixture, before the walls close.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fixtures and trim:</strong> the fixture and faucet allowance, then setting each fixture
      and installing faucets, valve trim, stops and traps after tile.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Water heater, testing and permits:</strong> the heater and what goes with it, the supply
      and drain tests, inspection trips and the permit.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Upgrades the customer may want, such as a recirculation pump or a repipe of the rest of the
    house, go in the optional-items table, priced but outside the total.
  </p>

  <h2>How to take off a plumbing job</h2>
  <p>
    Residential plumbing labor is counted by fixture. Each fixture gets two labor units, rough hours
    and trim hours, and each covers that fixture’s drain, vent and supply piping back to the main or
    stack. The Plumbing Takeoff sheet lists common fixtures with editable units. They are starting
    points, not a standard, so replace them with the hours your crews actually log. This is the
    schedule from the completed example:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr
        ><th>Fixture</th><th>Count</th><th>Rough hrs each</th><th>Trim hrs each</th><th>Hours</th
        ></tr
      >
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Water closet</td><td>1</td><td>4.0</td><td>1.5</td><td>5.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lavatory</td><td>2</td><td>3.0</td><td>1.5</td><td>9.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shower, valve and drain</td><td>1</td><td>5.0</td><td>1.0</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td>Freestanding tub, floor-mount filler</td><td>1</td><td>6.0</td><td>2.5</td><td>8.5</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td>5</td><td>21.0 rough</td><td>8.0 trim</td><td>29.0</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Demo, the water heater, the expansion tank, two tests and two inspection trips add 13.25 hours,
    for 42.25 in total. Pipe is taken off separately, by size: runs × average length gives footage,
    and waste plus the coil or stick length gives what to order. In the example, nine supply outlets
    at about 12 feet each come to 108 feet of 1/2" PEX, two 100-foot coils. The 3/4" mains add 68
    feet, one coil, and 86 feet of PVC drain and vent is 11 ten-foot sticks. Fittings, supports and
    cement are an allowance at 60% of pipe material, a figure to check against your own supplier
    invoices. Size drains, vents and supply to the code where the job is. Some jurisdictions adopt
    the IPC, others the UPC or a state code, often with local amendments.
  </p>

  <h2>Pricing plumbing line by line</h2>
  <p>
    Build each price from cost on the Unit Price Builder: material, plus labor hours at your fully
    loaded rate, plus consumables and disposal, then overhead, then profit as a
    <a href="/blog/construction-markup-vs-margin/">margin on price rather than a markup</a>. The
    example uses a $64 loaded rate, 30% overhead and a 20% margin, so labor sells at $104 an hour. A
    water closet rough-in at 4 hours, with $195 of material and $10 of consumables, comes out at
    $749.13 and is quoted at $750. On a remodel, price per fixture rather than per foot of pipe. The
    customer understands a toilet, not 12 feet of 3-inch PVC.
  </p>
  <p>
    The completed example is a primary bath remodel in a 1968 house outside Columbus, Ohio, plus a
    like-for-like 50-gallon gas water heater. Rough-in comes to $4,950, fixtures and trim to $3,650
    including the $2,400 allowance, and the water heater, expansion tank, tests, inspections and
    permits to $2,965. That is $11,565 in total. There is no tax line because the plumber pays tax
    on materials and includes it in the prices. The 30% deposit is $3,469.50, due on acceptance, and
    the $8,095.50 balance is due after the final inspection.
  </p>

  <h2>The fixture allowance, and what is behind the wall</h2>
  <p>
    Two things move a remodel plumbing price after it is signed: the owner’s fixture choices and the
    condition of what you tie into. Write both into the estimate. The fixture allowance states an
    amount for the toilet, faucets, shower trim and tub filler, and the terms say it adjusts at cost
    to what the owner picks. Get those selections before rough-in, because valve bodies are
    brand-specific. For the old piping, say what you replace and what you tie into, and exclude the
    rest. If opening the floor turns up a cracked cast-iron hub or rotted galvanized, write it up as
    a <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a> and get it signed before the work. For small
    unknowns billed by the hour, a signed <a href="/templates/tm-ticket/">T&amp;M ticket</a> works. When
    the job passes final inspection, bill the balance on an
    <a href="/templates/construction-invoice/">invoice</a> that references the estimate number and any
    approved changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Estimate, quote or proposal?</h2>
  <p>
    For most residential remodels and replacements, this estimate is the whole sale: a price, the
    terms and a signature. On new construction or a tenant improvement bid to a GC from drawings,
    build your cost on the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate worksheet</a> and send a
    <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">bid proposal</a> with inclusions, exclusions and alternates.
    Then bill against a <a href="/templates/schedule-of-values/">schedule of values</a> for underground,
    rough-in, top-out and trim. Our page for
    <a href="/solutions/plumbing-contractors/">plumbing contractors</a> covers how that work runs in BuildWorkPro,
    from the bid through pay applications.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes on plumbing estimates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      One price for the whole bathroom. Per-fixture lines show the customer what they are paying
      for, and make changes easy to price.
    </li>
    <li>
      Rough-in and trim priced as one trip. On a remodel they are weeks apart, and each visit costs
      a truck and travel time.
    </li>
    <li>No fixture allowance, and no word on who supplies the fixtures. Say which, in writing.</li>
    <li>
      Leaving out tests and inspection trips. They are real hours, and a failed inspection is
      another trip.
    </li>
    <li>No exclusion for drywall, tile and patching. Plumbers open walls; say who closes them.</li>
  </ul>
